another major story this weekend of huge  geopolitical and foreign policy  importance  is that long-time israeli prime minister  benjamin netanyahu of the likud  party is finally out as prime minister  replaced by the incoming prime minister  naftali bennett in israel much of what  takes place is coalition based and so  what we have seen here is that by  a single vote margin uh naftali bennett  is in and benjamin netanyahu is out  because of the coalition that bennett  was able to form the new york times  reports  the long and divisive reign of benjamin  netanyahu the dominant  israeli politician of the past  generation officially ended on sunday at  least for the time being  as the country's parliament gave its  vote of confidence to a precarious  coalition government  stitched together by widely disparate  anti-netanyahu forces  israelis israel's parliament the knesset  approved the new government by just a  single vote 60-59  with one abstention after his supporters  cheered the announcement of his election  naftali bennett then exchanged a brief  handshake with netanyahu  before walking to the rostrum at the  front of the parliamentary chamber  and taking the oath of office as prime  minister yair lapida centrist leader  is set to take mr bennett's place after  two years if their government can hold  together that long  they lead an eight-party alliance  ranging from left to right  from secular to religious that agrees on  little but a desire to oust netanyahu  the longest serving leader in the  country's history and to end  israel's lengthy political gridlock  in a speech made before the confidence  vote bennett hailed his unlikely  coalition  as an essential antidote to an  intractable stalemate  we stopped the train before the abyss  bennett said the time has come for  different leaders from all parts of the  people  to stop to stop this madness now you  will remember  that netanyahu as the article points out  and his right-wing allies from likud and  other far-right parties  did everything they could i i mean i  guess maybe there was something else  they could have done something  illegal i don't know they did almost  everything they could they applied  pressure  on other right-wing opposition lawmakers  they were trying to peel them away  uh from possibly being on the bennett  side but ultimately unable to  do it the question of course now is  what happens next it's important to  understand  that while uh naftali bennett is not  a likudnik uh the party likud of  benjamin netanyahu  there is the this political alliance  yamina  which is an alliance of right-wing  parties including  what was originally the new right uh and  and some other parties and we will see  ultimately how it shakes out in practice  now  it is still a right-wing coalition and  is still naftali bennett is still a  right-winger  um and it is a real question mark as to  as to what progress if any will be able  to be made  in terms of the israeli-palestinian  conflict now on the other side as i said  before  it is a requirement but it is necessary  but not sufficient  to remove benjamin netanyahu is there if  there is going to be any serious chance  at  peace talks that could be productive and  and permanent or semi-permanent as we  may call it  on the other side of that um hamas  we know simply cannot be  will not be they refuse to be there is  no way that hamas will be a party  to any kind of serious peace talks and  hamas seems potentially as ingrained as  ever  uh in gaza um hamas near if you want to  understand sort of the outrageous  dynamics of what's going on there  hamas recently awarded al jazeera an  award which al jazeera  gladly accepted for their coverage of  the recent  conflict there and for anybody who has  um  legitimate criticism of the way that the  recent flare up  with gaza was covered by certain  media outlets but does not have uh  concerns  about the coverage of for example al  jazeera which was recently given an  award by hamas  you're sort of missing half of the  picture so i remain extremely  skeptical as to the progress that will  be made in the next  two years serious progress when it comes  to the israeli-palestinian conflict but  removing benjamin netanyahu as prime  minister was step you know 0.1  it was a necessary step it is not  sufficient  and we will now see what happens next it  would be amazing  if there were able to be at least some  um  initial hypothetical guidelines set out  for some kind of serious path forward  but something that's important to  understand is  you know one of the risks of  casually following the internal politics  of other countries  is no matter who you follow like whether  you're following the new york times  and the times of israel or whether  you're following  al jazeera for your understanding of the  israeli-palestinian conflict  you sometimes miss the forest for the  trees and one of the things that is  really important to understand is that  within israel meaning to israelis  the israeli-palestinian conflict does  not actually rank particularly high  in terms of the concerns that the  israeli people have for what they want  a prime minister or the new prime  minister to focus on it's mostly  internal issues  it's internal issues related to  economics it's internal issues related  to  education and social services and  infrastructure  much like in the united states and it's  not different in that sense and this is  something that is really missed  when you look at israel and the  israeli-palestinian conflict  through any kind of western media  whether it's hard right or whether it's  hard left or whether it's centrist  our lens tends to be foreign policy of  israel and the israeli-palestinian  conflict  much like in the united states foreign  policy listen  in the middle of a completely  harebrained war with iraq  in 2004 uh george w bush  still was re-elected and the war in iraq  which  was like we went to war with the wrong  country over a lie 